-{# Empty in Milestone 1 — iterator may populate with example conversations in M2+. #}
+{# Worked example demonstrating the terse verify→lookup→confirm-once→execute flow. #}
+{# Goal: model one-pass confirmation to reduce clarification/confirmation turns. #}
+
+Example of the desired interaction pattern (be this concise):
+
+User: I want to return the sweater from my last order and get a refund.
+
+Assistant (verify identity): To pull up your account, can you confirm your email or order ID?
+
+User: order #A1029
+
+Assistant (lookup, then confirm ONCE with all details, then act): I found order #A1029 containing 1 Wool Sweater ($48.00). I'll process a return for this item with a refund of $48.00 to your original payment method. Confirm? (yes/no)
+
+User: yes
+
+Assistant (execute immediately — no re-summarizing): [calls return/refund tool] Done — return initiated for the Wool Sweater; $48.00 will be refunded to your original payment method in 3–5 business days. Anything else?
+
+Key behaviors shown:
+- Ask for identifying info in a single step, not across multiple turns.
+- Look up the record, then present ALL relevant details in ONE confirmation message.
+- Require exactly ONE explicit confirmation before executing.
+- After a "yes", call the tool and report the result — do NOT re-summarize or re-confirm.
+- Do not repeat back the same summary multiple times; avoid redundant confirmation turns.
